This batch contains comment-only changes.

EngineV2Bridge.swift: head lines 132-136 rewrite the kvBytesPerToken doc comment. It now says ordinary CBv2 slots price observed native per-layer K/V types via EngineV2SlotFactory.slotKVBytesPerToken, replacing the GPT-OSS fp32-delta description. No executable code changed. The init (head 348-485) is identical to base, including:
- SSD/complete-store acceptance or close (431-446);
- resident evidence gating;
- publication handlers.

EngineV2Factory+Configuration.swift: head 201-203 rewrites the nativeKVBytesPerToken doc comment. It now describes the function as a fallback for callers without observed native types and says it over-reserves bf16 rows. No logic change is visible.

Relevant threat: T-041, which lists EngineV2Bridge as an affected file. Its prefix-cache staging, salt scoping and SSD construction paths are untouched in these units. No change was found to:
- encryption or cache gating;
- attestation or authorization (TB-011);
- memory-budget enforcement.

Integration question: the comments imply that production now computes kvBytesPerToken from observed native per-layer K/V types in EngineV2SlotFactory.slotKVBytesPerToken. That code is not in this batch. If it underestimates actual residency, the result could be KV over-admission and memory exhaustion, an availability concern; a mixed or quantized type mapping is the specific risk. The claim that "production slots always supply them" is also unverified here. The next pass should verify that the slot factory's computed rate is at least the real per-token footprint and is used consistently by the GlobalKVCacheBudget reservations and heartbeats.
